[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) merged every report about a wrong date into one ticket and marked it
**known: one issue, one fix queued**. The reports do not all describe the same bug. The fix
that is queued repairs one of them. Reports about the other one are still coming in.

<Callout>
Merging was decision [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject): same subject, same issue. The
subjects are generic ("Date is wrong"), so they say nothing about the cause. The cause is in
the tenant: its locale and its booking form (`causes.ts`).
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitCounts ticket={4471} />

1. **The parser reads dates month first.** Day-first tenants get flipped dates. This is the
   bug the queued fix is for.
2. **The booking-v3 form tells day-first tenants to type `MM/DD/YYYY`.** Their people do as
   the hint says and the form reads it their way. The shared parser is not at fault, so the
   queued fix does not help. Form channel only; tenants on booking-v3 only.

Five reports describe a date that moves by one day, not a swap (Lisbon and Toronto got
different days; "only June"). The desk read them that way in `desk-notes.json`, but they are
counted where the desk files them, under the parser. They may point at a timezone bug. That
needs its own check, and it is not a third count here.

Each problem has its own ticket in the repo, with its report count:
[problem 1](https://github.com/LoicUV/kb-4471-split/issues/1), [problem 2](https://github.com/LoicUV/kb-4471-split/issues/2).

## Intake guessed wrong, often

`meta.intakeTag` is a guess from the reporter's browser. The tag disagrees with the real cause
for a share of the reports, so do not count by tag.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- Merging by subject counts the symptom. Two reports with one subject can have two causes.
- A count that has to be right comes from the data, after you drop duplicate ids.

<RenderStamp {signature} />
